1:["$","main",null,{"id":"main","className":"detail","children":[["$","a",null,{"className":"back","href":"/#research","children":"← All research"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"eyebrow","children":["RESEARCH / ","01"]}],["$","h1",null,{"children":"In disordered neural networks, finite is different"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"detail-subtitle","children":"Birth of sequential memory recall in finite delayed Hopfield networks"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"detail-meta","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":["University of Chicago",["$","br",null,{}],"March – August 2026"]}],["$","span",null,{"children":["Supervisor",["$","br",null,{}],"Vincenzo Vitelli · James Franck Institute"]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"status","children":"Research internship · 2026"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"links documents","children":[["$","a","/documents/hopfield-report.pdf",{"href":"/documents/hopfield-report.pdf","children":["Internship report · PDF"," ↗"]}],["$","a","https://github.com/leoF333/hopfield-dynamics",{"href":"https://github.com/leoF333/hopfield-dynamics","children":["Code, data & examples · GitHub"," ↗"]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"detail-grid","children":[["$","article",null,{"children":[["$","section","The system",{"children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"The system"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Symmetric couplings store memories. A delayed non-reciprocal drive orders them into a sequence. Finite disorder decides when the network stops being pinned and starts to move."}]]}],["$","section","The result",{"children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"The result"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Every memory ends at its own saddle-node threshold, forming a central-limit-like, near-Gaussian ensemble whose variance vanishes as the system size grows. The bulk destroys static recall, while the extreme value identifies the sequence bottleneck and predicts the dynamical onset of an invariant cycle."}]]}],["$","section","The method",{"children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"The method"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"An exact finite-sample reduction makes every memory branch accessible. Continuation, delay integration, Floquet spectra, Lyapunov analysis and finite-size statistics test the mechanism from independent directions."}]]}]]}],["$","aside",null,{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"research-note","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"note-label","children":"THE CENTRAL QUESTION"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"How is sequential memory recall born from static pattern retrieval in associative memory neural networks?"}]]}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"small-heading","children":"Methods"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"method-list","children":[["$","li","Exact low-rank dimensional reduction",{"children":"Exact low-rank dimensional reduction"}],["$","li","Continuation and stability analysis",{"children":"Continuation and stability analysis"}],["$","li","Delay-differential equations",{"children":"Delay-differential equations"}],["$","li","Seed-resolved validation and finite-size scaling",{"children":"Seed-resolved validation and finite-size scaling"}]]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"project-figure ","children":[["$","img",null,{"src":"/figures/hopfield-bifurcations.png","alt":"Memory branches ending at different thresholds, their distribution, and finite-size scaling in a delayed Hopfield network."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":"From local memory bifurcations to a distribution of thresholds. See the internship report and evidence register for protocols and qualifications."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"detail-end","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"Read the full argument, methods and references."}],["$","div",null,{"className":"links","children":[["$","a","/documents/hopfield-report.pdf",{"href":"/documents/hopfield-report.pdf","children":["Internship report · PDF"," ↗"]}],["$","a","https://github.com/leoF333/hopfield-dynamics",{"href":"https://github.com/leoF333/hopfield-dynamics","children":["Code, data & examples · GitHub"," ↗"]}]]}]]}]]}]
